-<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Does an HOA have to agree to a payment plan?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. South Carolina's HOA and condo laws do not make an association offer one. If it agrees to a plan, get the plan in writing, including what happens to the lawsuit."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you get your house back after an HOA foreclosure sale?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not by paying the association afterward. In a Murrells Inlet case, the owner offered the full fees as soon as he learned of the sale, and the association's lawyers turned it down because someone had already bid."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I stop paying HOA dues if the HOA is not doing its job?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. A condo owner cannot avoid the dues by not using the shared areas or by leaving the unit. In an HOA, paying the dues is a duty in the community's recorded rules. Holding them back starts the late charges, the lien and the lawsuit."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does bankruptcy wipe out HOA dues?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not the dues that fall due after you file. Those stay owed for as long as you still own the home."}}]}</script>
+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Does an HOA have to agree to a payment plan?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. South Carolina's HOA and condo laws do not make an association offer one. If it agrees to a plan, get the plan in writing, including what happens to the lawsuit."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you get your house back after an HOA foreclosure sale?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not by paying the association afterward. In a Murrells Inlet case, the owner offered the full fees as soon as he learned of the sale, and the association's lawyers turned it down because someone had already bid. The Supreme Court set the Winrose sale aside because the price was far too low, but that took five years."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I stop paying HOA dues if the HOA is not doing its job?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. A condo owner cannot avoid the dues by not using the shared areas or by leaving the unit. In an HOA, paying the dues is a duty in the community's recorded rules. Holding them back starts the late charges, the lien and the lawsuit."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can an HOA put a lien on your house for fines?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Only if your community's papers make fines part of the dues the lien covers. South Carolina's HOA law does not say."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does bankruptcy wipe out HOA dues?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not the dues that fall due after you file. Those stay owed for as long as you still own the home."}}]}</script>
