 cons of buying a condo in Myrtle Beach"}</script>
-<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you live in a Myrtle Beach condo and rent it out part of the year?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, but the home you live in loses the 4 percent tax rate if it is rented out more than 72 days in a calendar year. Then it is taxed at 6 percent, like any rental. An owner on active military duty under orders is the exception."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What do you pay the HOA when you buy a Myrtle Beach condo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually a transfer fee, and often a few months of dues paid ahead into escrow. Both are common in condo buildings here, with or without a rental program."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is it harder to sell a Myrtle Beach condo than a house?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually. At last year's pace of sales, the condos for sale now would take about a year to sell, and the houses and townhouses about 4 months. A condo owner should plan on more time to sell."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who owns the other units in a Myrtle Beach condo building?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Mostly investors and second-home owners. About 1 in 5 condo units here is taxed at the 4 percent rate for a home its owner lives in. Of the county's other homes, about 6 in 10 are."}}]}</script>
