isplay:block;width:100%;height:auto;aspect-ratio:var(--c3ph-r,4/3);object-fit:cover}.c3ph-card .c3ph-cr{font-size:.56rem;padding:1.2rem .45rem .25rem 1.6rem}.c3ph-card strong{display:block;color:var(--navy);font-size:.98rem;font-weight:500;line-height:1.3;padding:.7rem .8rem 0}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{display:block;color:var(--muted);font-size:.86rem;line-height:1.45;padding:.2rem .8rem .85rem}.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><style>section[style*="background:var(--ivory)"] .c3ph-card{background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-cards{max-width:920px}.c3ph-card strong{font-family:var(--sans)}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{font-size:1rem!important}.c3ph-card strong{font-size:1rem!important}ul[role=list] li span[style*="font-size:.9rem"]{font-size:1rem!important}section#c3-photo{padding-top:0}section:has(+section#c3-photo){background:var(--ivory-2)!important;padding-bottom:2rem}.c3ph-hero{margin:0}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero{max-width:900px;margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:auto!important;height:auto}.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:640px}.c3-aerial-ctop .c3ph-cr{top:0;bottom:auto;padding:.35rem .6rem 1.6rem 2.6rem;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 0%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0))}@media (max-width:699px){.c3ph-cards>:last-child:nth-child(odd){grid-column:1/-1}}.c3-maps{display:grid;gap:0 1.5rem;max-width:1136px}@media (min-width:900px){.c3-maps{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;align-items:start}}.c3-maps figure{max-width:none!important;margin:1.4rem 0 .4rem!important}.c3-maps svg,.c3-maps img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}.c3-map-links{row-gap:0!important}.c3-map-links a{padding:.55rem 0}ul[role=list][style*="minmax(13rem,1fr)"]{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(min(13rem,100%),1fr))!important}</style><style>.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero,.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:760px}</style><style>main#main .detail-hero .detail-sub{display:none}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. South Carolina's <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t40c057.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">license law</a> does not apply to an owner selling their own home, so you need no real estate license. This is called selling "for sale by owner", or FSBO.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Three duties apply to most owners who sell, with or without an agent:</p><ol class="fs-duties" aria-label="What every owner who sells must do in South Carolina"><li><b>A South Carolina attorney supervises the closing</b><a href="#attorney" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Who picks the attorney</a></li><li><b>The buyer gets the state's disclosure form before the contract</b><a href="#paperwork" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">What the form asks</a></li><li><b>Most homes built before 1978 need lead papers</b><a href="#lead" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Which homes</a></li></ol></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="what-you-save"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What you save">How much do you save selling by owner on a Myrtle Beach-area home?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You save the listing broker's fee, what you would pay an agent to market and sell the home. Try your own numbers:</p><div class="fs-calc" role="group" aria-labelledby="fsCalcT"><p class="k" id="fsCalcT">Try your own price</p><div class="fs-in"><label for="fsPrice">Your price</label><span class="u">$ <input id="fsPrice" type="number" min="0" step="1000" value="350000" oninput="fsCalc()"></span></div><div class="fs-in"><label for="fsPct">A fee you were quoted, in percent</label><span class="u"><input id="fsPct" type="number" min="0" max="10" step="0.25" value="1" oninput="fsCalc()"> %</span></div><dl class="fs-out"><div><dt>The fee you do not pay</dt><dd id="fsFee">$3,500</dd></div><div><dt>Deed fee, owed either way</dt><dd id="fsDeed">$1,295</dd></div></dl><p class="n">The box starts at 1 percent. Type the fee an agent quoted you.</p></div><script>function fsCalc(){var g=function(i){var v=parseFloat(document.getElementById(i).value);return isFinite(v)&&v>0?v:0};var m=function(n){return "$"+Math.round(n).toLocaleString("en-US")};var p=g("fsPrice"),f=g("fsPct");document.getElementById("fsDeed").textContent=m(Math.ceil(p/500)*1.85);document.getElementById("fsFee").textContent=m(p*f/100)}</script><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two costs stay the same with or without an agent. You owe the deed fee, also called <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c024.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">deed stamps</a>: $1.85 for each $500 of the price. You also pay your share of the closing attorney's fee. Ask the attorney's office for its fee in writing before the contract says who pays it.</p><style>.c3-cc{margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:960px}.c3-cc table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--muted)}.c3-cc caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;line-height:1.4;padding:0 0 .7rem}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);overflow-wrap:break-word}.c3-cc thead th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);vertical-align:bottom}.c3-cc tbody th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}.c3-cc .n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.c3-cc .i{display:inline-flex;vertical-align:-4px;margin-right:.45rem;color:var(--brass-ink)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:#fff}@media (max-width:49.999em){.c3-cc table,.c3-cc tbody,.c3-cc tr,.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{display:block;width:auto}.c3-cc caption{display:block}.c3-cc thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}.c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);margin:0 0 .8rem;padding:.5rem 0 .6rem}section[style*="background:var(--ivory-2)"] .c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self{background:#fff!important;border:2px solid var(--brass)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:transparent}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{border:0;padding:.2rem 1rem}.c3-cc tbody th{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:1.1em;padding-bottom:.35rem}.c3-cc tbody th .i{flex:none;vertical-align:0}.c3-cc tbody th a{display:inline-block;padding:.5rem 0}.c3-cc .n{text-align:left}.c3-cc td[data-label]::before{content:attr(data-label) ": ";color:var(--navy);font-weight:500}}</style><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>Three ways to sell a $350,000 home, and what each one costs you</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">How you sell</th><th scope="col">How it works</th><th scope="col">Listing broker's fee</th><th scope="col">Buyer's agent</th><th scope="col">Coaching fee</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row"><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M10.8 12.2 20 3M16.8 6.2l2.5 2.5M14.3 8.7l2 2"/><circle cx="7.5" cy="15.5" r="4.5"/></svg></span>Sell it yourself</th><td data-label="How it works">You do all of it</td><td data-label="Listing broker's fee">None</td><td data-label="Buyer's agent">Your choice</td><td data-label="Coaching fee">None</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5.5 7h8A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 15 8.5v11a1.5 1.5 0 0 1-1.5 1.5h-8A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-11A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 5.5 7zM8 7V4.5A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 9.5 3h9A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 20 4.5v11a1.5 1.5 0 0 1-1.5 1.5H15M9.5 17.5l-2.3-2.4a1.4 1.4 0 0 1 2.3-1.6 1.4 1.4 0 0 1 2.3 1.6z"/></svg></span>Sell it yourself, with our coaching</th><td data-label="How it works">You do it, we coach</td><td data-label="Listing broker's fee">None</td><td data-label="Buyer's agent">Your choice</td><td data-label="Coaching fee">Flat fee, credited if you list with us</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 11.5 12 3.5l9 8M5.5 10v10.5h13V10M10 20.5V15h4v5.5"/></svg></span>List with an agent</th><td data-label="How it works">The agent markets and sells it</td><td data-label="Listing broker's fee">What you agree with your broker</td><td data-label="Buyer's agent">Your choice</td><td data-label="Coaching fee">None</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our <a href="/sell/net-proceeds/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">net proceeds calculator</a> works out what you keep after your loan payoff.</p><h3 id="sell-for-less" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do houses sold by owner sell for less?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Nationally, yes, but more than half of by-owner sellers already knew the buyer. These are the middle prices in <a href="https://nar.realtor/newsroom/first-time-home-buyer-share-falls-to-historic-low-of-21-median-age-rises-to-40" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the 2025 survey</a> by NAR, the National Association of Realtors:</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="Middle sale price in NAR's 2025 survey, by how the home sold"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Middle sale price in NAR's 2025 survey, by how the home sold</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">How it sold</span><span role="columnheader">middle price</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">By owner, seller knew the buyer</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.847" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$360,000</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">By owner, seller did not know the buyer</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.876" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$372,200</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Sold with an agent</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$425,000</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>The groups sell different homes in different places, so the gap is not all price lost. The survey covers people who bought a home to live in and also sold one.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We read a year of county deeds, and they point the same way for sales between people who share a last name:</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="Horry County home sales recorded under $100,000"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Horry County home sales recorded under $100,000</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Who sold to whom, how many</span><span role="columnheader">share under $100,000</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Seller and buyer share a last name</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">22 of 73 sales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">3 in 10</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Other sales between people</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">294 of 7,449 sales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.131" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">1 in 25</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Resales between two people, with no company, trust, estate or builder on either side. A shared last name is not proof of family, and relatives with different last names are missed. The middle price was $200,000 when the two shared a last name and $310,000 for the rest.</figcaption></figure><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">What would you keep, by owner or with an agent?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send the address. We price the home from recorded county sales and work out what you keep both ways, free.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="#lead-form">Have us work out what you keep</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="attorney"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The attorney">Do you need a lawyer to sell a house by owner in South Carolina?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, for the closing, the meeting where the buyer pays and you sign the house over. Every home closing in South Carolina <a href="https://www.sccourts.org/media/opinions/HTMLFiles/SC/25508.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">must be supervised</a> by a South Carolina attorney.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Anyone who prepares your deed for you, the paper that transfers the house, must be a lawyer.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Who picks the closing attorney?</h3><ul class="fs-cards fs-3" aria-label="Who chooses the closing attorney"><li><span class="k">The buyer has a home loan</span><b>The buyer chooses</b>If the loan is for a home the buyer will live in, the buyer picks the closing attorney, and the lender must follow that choice.</li><li><span class="k">Every sale</span><b>The contract can name one</b>The contract can name the closing attorney and say who pays the attorney's fees. You and the buyer agree on it.</li><li class="hot"><span class="k">You want advice</span><b>Hire your own</b>An attorney who represents only the buyer does not advise the seller. Advice on your side of the deal comes from your own attorney.</li></ul><h3 id="contract" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Who writes the purchase contract?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A South Carolina attorney can write the contract for you, or read the buyer's before you sign. If the buyer has an agent, the offer may come on a printed form an attorney wrote. A 2007 <a href="https://www.scag.gov/wp-content/uploads/2011/03/harrison-j-h-5-22-07-os-8395-unauthorized-practice-of-law.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">opinion</a> of the state Attorney General's office said an agent filling in its blanks does not appear to practice law, if no legal advice is given.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="paperwork"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The paperwork">What paperwork do you need to sell by owner in South Carolina?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The main paper is the state's <a href="https://llr.sc.gov/re/recpdf/Property-Condition-Disclosure-Statement-06.2025.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Residential Property Condition Disclosure Statement</a>, a form where you tell the buyer what you know about the home. <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c050.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">State law</a> requires it for a home with one to four units, unless the sale is one of those <a href="#no-form" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">listed below</a>. The form is free.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">It has nine parts and 28 numbered questions.</p><figure class="fs-fig"><p class="cap">What South Carolina's disclosure form asks, part by part</p><ol class="fs-form" aria-label="The nine parts of the South Carolina disclosure form"><li><span class="pn">I</span><span class="pt">Water and sewer</span><span class="pq">Questions 1 to 4</span></li><li><span class="pn">II</span><span class="pt">Roof, gutters, foundation and floors</span><span class="pq">Questions 5 to 7</span></li><li><span class="pn">III</span><span class="pt">Plumbing, electrical, appliances, heating and cooling</span><span class="pq">Questions 8 to 14</span></li><li><span class="pn">IV</span><span class="pt">Termites, wood rot and other pests</span><span class="pq">Describe</span></li><li class="hot"><span class="pn">V</span><span class="pt">Zoning, rights of others to use your land, legal claims, water damage, flood and the beach</span><span class="pq">Questions 15 to 28</span><span class="coast"><b>Questions 22 to 28, on the coast and flooding</b><ul><li><i>22</i>Erosion and erosion control</li><li><i>23</i>Flood hazards, wetlands, flood zones, flood risk</li><li><i>24</i>Flood insurance on the property now</li><li><i>25</i>Flood insurance claims during your ownership</li><li><i>26</i>Flood repairs not claimed on insurance during your ownership</li><li><i>27</i>Federal flood disaster aid during your ownership</li><li><i>28</i>A charge for beach nourishment, sand added to the beach, while you owned it</li></ul>Question 20 also asks whether the house flooded while you owned it.</span></li><li><span class="pn">VI</span><span class="pt">Lead paint, asbestos, radon, buried tanks and mold</span><span class="pq">Describe</span></li><li><span class="pn">VII</span><span class="pt">Leases and vacation rentals in place at closing</span><span class="pq">Describe</span></li><li><span class="pn">VIII</span><span class="pt">Utility charges and equipment the utility financed</span><span class="pq">Describe</span></li><li><span class="pn">IX</span><span class="pt">Whether the home is in an association</span><span class="pq">Yes or No, then the addendum</span></li></ol><figcaption>Our drawing of the state's form. Each numbered question is answered Yes, No or No Representation. Parts marked "Describe" have lines to write on.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A home in a homeowners association (HOA), a property owners association or a condo also needs the form's addendum, with 11 more questions. Our <a href="/hoa/documents/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">HOA documents page</a> lists the association papers a buyer will ask for.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Why the flood questions matter here</h3><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/sell-fsbo/raised-beach-houses-surfside-beach-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/sell-fsbo/raised-beach-houses-surfside-beach-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/sell-fsbo/raised-beach-houses-surfside-beach-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="675" alt="Rows of two- and three-story beach houses behind the dunes, a mid-rise building and parking lots beside the Surfside Beach fishing pier, seen from over the ocean" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="USGS photo, 2011. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">USGS photo, 2011</a></span></div><figcaption>Beach houses beside the Surfside Beach pier in 2011. Questions 22 to 28 on the state form ask every seller about erosion, flooding and beach nourishment.</figcaption></figure></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">FEMA has paid 8,815 flood insurance claims in Horry County since 1978. Nearly 1 in 4 were on buildings rated outside the high-risk flood zone. That is why questions 23 to 27 matter here, even away from the water. Our <a href="/buyers/undisclosed-flooding/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">flood history page</a> shows what a buyer can find out.</p><h3 id="form-rules" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Three rules about the form</h3><ul class="fs-cards fs-3" aria-label="Three rules about the disclosure form"><li><span class="k">"No Representation"</span><b>Not a way to skip a question</b>It means you do not know enough to answer Yes or No. It does not protect you if you know of the problem.</li><li><span class="k">After you hand it over</span><b>Correct it if anything changes</b>If the roof starts to leak before closing, you must correct the form or repair the roof.</li><li class="hot"><span class="k">If you leave something out</span><b>You can owe the buyer</b>A seller who knowingly leaves a problem off the form, or answers falsely, can owe the buyer their actual losses and court costs. A court may add the winner's attorney fees.</li></ul><h3 id="no-form" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Sales that need no form</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina lists 15 kinds of sale that need no form. Among them:</p><ul style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">A sale only to your spouse, or only to your children, grandchildren, parents or grandparents. If your child's spouse is also a buyer, the sale needs the form.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">A sale only between co-owners.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">A sale by an executor or trustee settling an estate or a trust.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">Any sale where buyer and seller both agree in writing to skip the form.</li></ul><div class="fs-lead-box" id="lead"><b>Homes built before 1978</b>A seller of most homes built before 1978 must also give the buyer the <a href="https://www.epa.gov/lead/real-estate-disclosures-about-potential-lead-hazards" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">federal lead pamphlet</a>. You also say what you know about lead paint and hand over any lead reports. The contract must include a lead warning statement. The buyer gets 10 days to test for lead, unless both agree in writing to a different period or the buyer waives it. Keep the signed form for three years. Four cases are not covered. One is a unit with no bedroom. Another is housing for older people or people with disabilities where no child under six lives. The others are a home a certified inspector found free of lead paint, and a foreclosure sale.</div><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Want a licensed agent to read your form first?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send us your filled-in form before a buyer sees it. Our agents read every answer and mark the ones a buyer will ask about.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="#lead-form">Send us your form to review</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="buyers"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Finding buyers">How will buyers find your house, and do you have to pay their agent?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Owners who sell by themselves reach buyers with a yard sign, word of mouth, open houses and listing websites. In <a href="https://www.rirealtors.org/clientuploads/documents/NAR/Homebuyers_and_sellers_trend_2025.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NAR's 2025 survey</a>, by-owner sellers did it these ways:</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="How owners who sold by themselves reached buyers"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">How owners who sold by themselves reached buyers</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Way they reached buyers</span><span role="columnheader">share of by-owner sellers</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">A yard sign</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.6" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">24%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Friends, relatives or neighbors</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.55" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">22%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">The MLS website</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.5" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">20%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">An open house</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.5" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">20%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Realtor.com</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.35" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">14%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Sites that gather listings from many sources</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.325" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">13%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Social media</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.225" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">9%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">For-sale-by-owner websites</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.125" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">5%</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Did not actively market it</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">40%</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>NAR's 2025 survey. Sellers could name more than one way, so the shares add to more than 100 percent.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On the MLS, your house competes with all of these:</p><div class="fs-stat"><p class="k">Houses for sale in Horry County now</p><ul><li><strong>About 3,700</strong><span>houses listed for sale on the MLS, the database brokers share</span></li><li><strong>56 percent</strong><span>of them have already cut their asking price. Buyers compare yours with all of them.</span></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="https://llr.sc.gov/re/RECPDF/Limited%20Market%20Exposure%20Guidance%20Document.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Broad marketing</a> generally exposes a home to the greatest number of qualified buyers. Keeping a home off the open market may reduce buyer competition and may hurt the price, terms or time to sell.</p><h3 id="buyers-agent" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do you have to pay the buyer's agent?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No rule requires it, and no law names an amount. Since August 2024, an agent who uses the MLS <a href="https://www.nar.realtor/the-facts/what-the-nar-settlement-means-for-home-buyers-and-sellers" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">signs a written agreement</a> with a buyer before touring a home. Offers to pay that agent are no longer made on the MLS.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You can still offer to pay the buyer's agent, or offer the buyer a credit toward their costs.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">A price from the sales next to yours</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We pull the recorded sales closest to your home and send you a price range, free, whether you sell by owner or list.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="#lead-form">Have us find the sales closest to yours</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="steps"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The steps">What are the steps to sell a house by owner in South Carolina?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Eight steps, from the price to the money in your account. Steps marked "Required by law" come from state or federal rules.</p><ol class="fs-steps" aria-label="The steps to sell a house by owner in South Carolina, in order"><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">1</span><span class="c"><span class="t">Before you list</span><b>Set the price</b>Price it from the recorded sales most like your home. Our <a href="/sell/home-value/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">home value page</a> shows how.</span></li><li class="hot"><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span class="c"><span class="t">Before you list</span><span class="t law">Required by law</span><b>Fill in the papers</b>The <a href="#paperwork" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">state's form</a>, any HOA addendum and any <a href="#lead" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">lead papers</a>.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">3</span><span class="c"><span class="t">Showings</span><b>Reach buyers and show the house</b>Keep valuables and medicines out of sight during showings.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">4</span><span class="c"><span class="t">Before you sign</span><b>Check that the buyer can pay</b>Ask for a loan approval letter from a lender, or proof of the cash, before you sign anything.</span></li><li class="hot"><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">5</span><span class="c"><span class="t">Signing</span><span class="t law">Required by law</span><b>Hand over the form, then sign the contract</b>The form comes first, unless the contract names another time. Agree in writing on the deposit and who holds it, the inspection period, the loan deadline and the closing date. <a href="#contract" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Who writes the contract</a></span></li><li class="hot"><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">6</span><span class="c"><span class="t">After signing</span><span class="t law">Required by law</span><b>The buyer's lead test</b>For most homes built before 1978, the buyer's <a href="#lead" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">lead test period</a> starts now.</span></li><li class="hot"><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">7</span><span class="c"><span class="t">Before closing</span><span class="t law">Required by law</span><b>Keep the form up to date</b>The <a href="#form-rules" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">form rules</a> apply until closing day.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">8</span><span class="c"><span class="t">Closing day</span><b>Sign, and get paid by wire</b><a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/archive/blog/mortgage-closing-scams-how-protect-yourself-and-your-closing-funds/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Scammers send fake wiring instructions</a> by email. Confirm them by phone with the attorney's office, at a number you agreed on in advance.</span></li></ol><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our <a href="/sell/capital-gains/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">capital gains page</a> and our <a href="/sell/out-of-state-buyers/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">page for sellers out of state</a> cover tax on the sale. If you live in another state, the buyer holds back 5.21 percent of a 2026 sale for state tax, figured on your gain if you sign Form I-295.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="lead-form"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Our coaching">What does Chapter3's coaching for owners include?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You list and sell the home yourself, and keep what you save. A licensed Chapter3 agent coaches you through it for a flat fee:</p><ul class="fs-coach" aria-label="What the coaching covers"><li>The price, from county sales of similar homes</li><li>The state's form, read before a buyer sees it</li><li>The purchase contract and its deadlines</li><li>Checking a buyer's loan approval or proof of cash</li><li>Lining up the closing attorney and closing day</li><li>Whether listing is worth the fee for your house</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If you decide to list with us at any point, the full coaching fee is credited back. The price check is free either way.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We are paid the flat coaching fee if you sell it yourself. If you list with us, we are paid the listing fee you agree with us, less the coaching fee you already paid.</p><div id="ldWrap" class="fs-form-box"><div class="idx-lead" style="grid-template-columns:1fr"><input class="idx-input" id="ldCtx" placeholder="Property address" aria-label="Property address" autocomplete="street-address"></div><div class="idx-lead"><input class="idx-input" id="ldName" placeholder="Your name" aria-label="Your name" autocomplete="name"><input class="idx-input" id="ldPhone" placeholder="Phone" aria-label="Phone" type="tel" autocomplete="tel"></div><div class="idx-lead" style="grid-template-columns:1fr"><input class="idx-input" id="ldEmail" placeholder="Email" aria-label="Email" type="email" autocomplete="email"></div><label class="fs-consent"><input type="checkbox" id="ldConsent"><span>I consent to receive calls and text messages from Chapter 3 Realty about my property inquiry, showing appointments, and listing information I requested, at the phone number provided, including calls placed using an automated system or an artificial or prerecorded voice. Message frequency varies. Message and data rates may apply. Reply HELP for help, STOP to opt out. Consent is not a condition of any purchase.</span></label><p id="ldErr" class="fs-err" role="alert"></p><button type="button" class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" onclick="ldSubmit()">Send my coaching request</button><p class="fs-tel">Free, no obligation. Prefer to talk now? Call <a href="tel:+18543332135">854.333.2135</a>.</p></div><div id="ldOk" class="fs-ok">Thanks. A licensed team member will reach out the same day, evenings included, to explain the coaching and build your free pricing analysis.</div><script>function ldSubmit(){var cx=document.getElementById('ldCtx').value.trim(),n=document.getElementById('ldName').value.trim(),ph=document.getElementById('ldPhone').value.trim(),em=document.getElementById('ldEmail').value.trim(),c=document.getElementById('ldConsent').checked,err=document.getElementById('ldErr');function fail(m){err.textContent=m;err.style.display='block';}err.style.display='none';if(!cx||!n){fail('Add the property address and your name.');return;}if(!ph&&!em){fail('Add a phone number or an email so we can reach you.');return;}if(!c){fail('Check the consent box to send the request.');return;}c3SendForm({property_address:cx,name:n,phone:ph,email:em,consent:c?'yes':'no'},'fsbo-coaching');document.getElementById('ldWrap').style.display='none';document.getElementById('ldOk').style.display='block';}</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="selling-by-owner-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Selling by owner FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can I put my house on the MLS without an agent?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Only through a broker, since the MLS is the database real estate brokers share. In NAR's 2025 survey, 7 percent of sellers who used an agent said the agent put the home on the MLS and did little else.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do I have to tell a buyer that someone died in the house?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. South Carolina does not require it. The same goes for the sex offender registry, and an illness highly unlikely to spread by living in the home. If a buyer asks you directly, you may not lie.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can I sell my house as is in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. "As is" means without repairs. An as-is sale still needs the state's disclosure form, unless it is a sale that needs none, such as some family sales.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do I pay tax when I sell my house by owner?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Often not. Under federal tax law, you can leave up to $250,000 of gain out of your income, or $500,000 for a married couple filing jointly. You must have owned and lived in the home at least 2 of the last 5 years.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t40c057.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 40-57</a>, <a href="https://www.sccourts.org/media/opinions/HTMLFiles/SC/25508.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Doe v. McMaster</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t37c010.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 37-10</a>, <a href="https://www.scbar.org/for-lawyers/quicklinks/legal-resources/ethics-advisory-opinions/ethics-advisory-opinion-06-11/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Bar 06-11</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c050.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-50</a>, <a href="https://llr.sc.gov/re/recpdf/Property-Condition-Disclosure-Statement-06.2025.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC form</a>, <a href="https://www.epa.gov/lead/real-estate-disclosures-about-potential-lead-hazards" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EPA lead rule</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c024.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-24</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c008.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-8</a>, <a href="https://dor.sc.gov/news/information-about-h-4216" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SCDOR H. 4216</a>, <a href="https://nar.realtor/newsroom/first-time-home-buyer-share-falls-to-historic-low-of-21-median-age-rises-to-40" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NAR 2025 release</a>, <a href="https://www.rirealtors.org/clientuploads/documents/NAR/Homebuyers_and_sellers_trend_2025.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NAR 2025 Profile</a>, <a href="https://www.scag.gov/wp-content/uploads/2011/03/harrison-j-h-5-22-07-os-8395-unauthorized-practice-of-law.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC AG opinion</a>, <a href="https://www.nar.realtor/the-facts/what-the-nar-settlement-means-for-home-buyers-and-sellers" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NAR settlement</a>, <a href="https://llr.sc.gov/re/RECPDF/Limited%20Market%20Exposure%20Guidance%20Document.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Commission</a>, <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">County deeds</a>, <a href="https://www.fema.gov/openfema-data-page/fima-nfip-redacted-claims-v2" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenFEMA</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc701" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Topic 701</a>, <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/archive/blog/mortgage-closing-scams-how-protect-yourself-and-your-closing-funds/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CFPB</a>. Our reading of county deeds: the $350,000 home; the last-name count, October 2025 to September 2026. Listings: our search, in aggregate.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Legal notice.</strong> Chapter3 Realty Corp is a licensed South Carolina real estate brokerage. We are not attorneys, and nothing on this page is legal or tax advice. For advice on your own sale, talk to a South Carolina attorney.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Rows of two- and three-story beach houses behind the dunes, a mid-rise building and parking lots beside the Surfside Beach fishing pier, seen from over the ocean. Photo: U.S. Geological Survey, <a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Geological Survey</a>.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/sell-fsbo/raised-beach-houses-surfside-beach-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/sell-fsbo/raised-beach-houses-surfside-beach-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":675,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"Rows of two- and three-story beach houses behind the dunes, a mid-rise building and parking lots beside the Surfside Beach fishing pier, seen from over the ocean","caption":"Beach houses beside the Surfside Beach pier in 2011. Questions 22 to 28 on the state form ask every seller about erosion, flooding and beach nourishment.","description":"Rows of two- and three-story beach houses behind the dunes, a mid-rise building and parking lots beside the Surfside Beach fishing pier, seen from over the ocean","creator":{"@type":"Organization","name":"U.S. Geological Survey"},"creditText":"U.S","copyrightNotice":"Public domain (U.S. Geological Survey)","license":"https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/","acquireLicensePage":"https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX","isBasedOn":"https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX","contentLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"The pier and the oceanfront of the Town of Surfside Beach (Horry County tax district 660), Surfside Beach, South Carolina"},"representativeOfPage":true}]}</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Sell it yourself, with a licensed agent's help</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.75);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:1.6rem">Coaching for a flat fee, credited back in full if you list with us. Start with a free price from recorded county sales.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="#lead-form">Ask us about coaching</a></div></section></main>
